// NO `expect-` MARKER IS SPELLABLE FOR THIS FILE, AND THAT IS THE DEFECT.
// `npkc` ACCEPTS it at exit 0 and `llc` then refuses the IR, so there is
// neither an exit code nor a diagnostic to expect. It is named in
// `harness/run.py`'s `EXPECT_EXEMPT` with that reason, which is the only
// honest bucket the sweep has for it (TM-115, V-1c). When the defect lands
// the marker becomes `// expect-exit: 0`, the exemption goes, and the sweep's
// both-directions diff makes removing it compulsory rather than optional.
//
// THE DEFECT (O-N17, raised 2026-09-05 at pin `0dfddac`).
//
// A GENERIC function that MOVES OUT OF an indexed element, instantiated at an
// OWNING `T`, emits a call to a `@npk.vacant.<dty>` helper whose body is never
// defined:
//
//     npkc  -> exit 0, writes the .ll
//     llc   -> exit 1, "use of undefined value '@npk.vacant.1876'"
//
// The emitted IR, verbatim, from the `T = string` instantiation:
//
//     %t6  = getelementptr { ptr, i64, i64 }, ptr %t4, i64 %t5
//     %t7  = load { ptr, i64, i64 }, ptr %t6
//     store { ptr, i64, i64 } zeroinitializer, ptr %t6
//     call void @"npk.vacant.1876"(ptr %t6)          ; <- no `define` anywhere
//
// The LAYOUT is substituted correctly -- the `getelementptr` is over
// `{ptr,i64,i64}`, a `string` -- so only the vacancy helper's type id is
// wrong or unregistered. `@npk.drop.1876` is never referenced either, and no
// body for 1876 is emitted; the module defines five `npk.vacant.*` bodies and
// makes five `npk.vacant.*` calls, and the two sets differ by this one.
//
// THE COMPILER'S OWN INVARIANT SAYS THIS CANNOT HAPPEN. Read at the pin,
// `src/backend/ir/ir_types.npk`, `drop_type_register`'s header (D-225):
//
//     ONE REGISTRATION LIST, TWO BODIES PER ENTRY (D-225). Every registered
//     type gets both a `@"npk.drop.<dty>"` and a `@"npk.vacant.<dty>"` [...]
//     the pair is the invariant, and splitting the registration is what keeps
//     them from drifting apart.
//
// and `vacant_fn_sym` calls `drop_type_register` before returning the symbol,
// precisely so a referenced symbol is a registered one. So the reference here
// reached the IR by a route that did not register the type -- cited by SYMBOL
// rather than by line, because our pin and the compiler's tree differ.
//
// WHY IT WAS NEVER HIT BEFORE, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING. The prelude's
// `List<T>` -- the shape `BUILD.md` B-12 says has "been exercised across
// twenty-two families" -- has exactly THREE public functions at this pin:
// `list_init`, `list_reserve`, `list_push`. There is no `list_pop`, no
// `list_at`, no `list_set` and no `list_free`. Every move OUT of an element in
// the compiler's own tree is in a CONCRETE function -- `src/frontend/
// diagnostics.npk`'s `Diagnostic:cur = move(list.items[i])` over a
// `List<Diagnostic>` -- which is `case2` below, and which links. So the
// exercised shape is push-only, and `0.0.4.md` §2's `vec_pop<T>` is the first
// consumer of the unexercised half.
//
// WHAT IT BLOCKS HERE: `vec_pop<T>` at an owning `T`, and with it the cycle
// checklist's "exercised at both `T` shapes" for that one function.
// Everything else in `0.0.4.md` §2's table is unaffected -- `case4` shows the
// move IN is fine, and `case3` shows a scalar `T` is fine.
mod:case1_generic_move_out;

struct:Vec<T> = { wild T->:items; int64:count; int64:cap; };

// The one function under test. Nothing else in this file is load-bearing.
func:vec_pop<T> = T(Vec<T>->:v) never fails {
    v.count = v.count - 1i64;
    pass move(v.items[v.count]);
};

func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    wild int8->:mem = alloc(2i64 * #size_of<string>());
    Vec<string>:b = Vec{ items: mem =>! wild string->, count: 0i64, cap: 2i64 };
    b.items[0i64] = string_concat("Europe/", "Kyiv");
    b.count = 1i64;
    string:owned = raw vec_pop(@b);
    if (string_byte_length(owned) != 11i64) { exit 12i32; }
    dalloc(b.items =>! wild int8->);
    exit 0i32;
};

func:failsafe = int32(Error:e) {
    pick (e) {
        (HeapBadRequest) { exit 91i32; },
        (HeapOom)        { exit 92i32; },
        (IntOverflow)    { exit 93i32; },
        (OutOfBounds)    { exit 94i32; },
        (Unreachable)    { exit 95i32; },
        (WildLeak)       { exit 96i32; },
        (*)              { exit 99i32; }
    }
    exit 9i32;
};
